# Relationships and program

**Improved question:** Which activities must happen in strict sequence within each dish/station, which can run in parallel on different burners or during idle prep time, which must finish before a dependent step can start, and critically—when must the rice be ready relative to the stir-fry so both are hot and plated together at the earliest moment? Should any prep step be held or delayed to synchronize with the cooking phases?

**Answer:** The rice track (rinse → boil → simmer → steam → fluff) is the longest sequential chain on a burner and sets the overall makespan. Prep work (slice chicken, marinate, prep vegetables, make sauce) happens in parallel on the prep station and must be complete before stir-frying begins. The stir-fry sequence (heat wok → sear chicken → steam broccoli → add aromatics → finish with sauce) occupies the second burner and must start *after* the rice enters its steam phase (when the first burner is free). The chicken marination is a timed hold—it must sit 10 minutes before stir-frying, so slicing should not be rushed. The rice fluff should be the very last step, timed to happen as the stir-fry finishes, so both are hot at plate time. No step is inferred beyond the text.

**Revised triggers:**
- Rice track starts at programStart; each step chains afterStep.
- Prep work starts at programStart in parallel; marinating is a 10-minute hold after slicing, not active work.
- Stir-fry (wok heat) begins when rice enters steam phase (afterStep simmer-rice, accounting for the 15m simmer duration).
- Fluff-rice happens afterStep finish-stir-fry so both finish together.

